<BaseLayout title="Wildfire Systems — Julian Nicholls" description="Wildfire science, remote sensing, simulation, GIS, and scientific software experience.">
  <article class="case shell">
    <header class="case-hero wildfire-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Flagship case study / wildfire systems</div><h1>From fire observations to operational decisions.</h1><p>My wildfire work spans remote sensing, monitoring-system simulation, raster scientific software, fire-danger calculations, and decision-support systems built for people managing real incidents and landscapes.</p></header>
    <div class="case-summary"><div><span>Domain</span><strong>Wildfire science + operations</strong></div><div><span>Methods</span><strong>GIS · simulation · remote sensing</strong></div><div><span>Software</span><strong>C++ · Python · raster systems</strong></div></div>
    <section class="case-section two-col"><div><div class="section-label">01 / The thread</div><h2>Scientific models become useful when they survive contact with operations.</h2></div><div><p>My early wildfire work taught me to think about software as a chain of evidence: observations and historical data feed models; models need numerical and scientific validation; outputs must stay spatially legible; and operational users need to understand what a system is telling them well enough to act.</p><p>That is still the architecture I reach for today.</p></div></section>
    <section class="case-section"><div class="section-label">02 / Work across the stack</div><div class="capability-grid"><div><h3>Remote sensing</h3><p>Worked with wildfire monitoring and remote-sensing problems, including near-real-time fire observations and literature/research on sensing technologies.</p></div><div><h3>Simulation</h3><p>Graduate research modeled high-altitude, long-endurance UAV concepts against historical Northern Ontario wildfire activity, including operational and cost considerations.</p></div><div><h3>Fire behaviour software</h3><p>Maintained and developed raster-based C++ scientific software and unit tests, including Canadian Forest Fire Danger Rating System calculations.</p></div><div><h3>Decision support</h3><p>Built and communicated systems in an applied fire-management context, with attention to explainability, validation, and operational usability.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section timeline-section"><div class="section-label">03 / Progression</div><div class="timeline"><div><span>Observe</span><h3>Remote sensing + fire detection</h3><p>Environmental observations become structured spatial inputs.</p></div><div><span>Model</span><h3>UAV simulation + fire behaviour</h3><p>Historical incidents and fire-science calculations become testable models.</p></div><div><span>Decide</span><h3>Spatial Fire Management System</h3><p>Raster calculations and GIS become operational decision-support surfaces.</p></div><div><span>Communicate</span><h3>Training + technical presentations</h3><p>Scientific systems only work when domain users can interrogate and trust them.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section closing-note"><div><div class="section-label">Why it matters now</div><h2>The domain changed. The systems problem didn't.</h2></div><p>TerrOps now applies the same pattern to weather-aware field operations: ingest heterogeneous environmental data, engineer spatial facts, estimate workload, plan constrained work, preserve evidence, and expose enough reasoning for humans to stay in control.</p></section>
  </article>
